00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.030 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  welcome back to cbs this morning  many employers around the country are  grappling with how to get workers back  safely in this pandemic  epic systems is a healthcare software  company that is the largest private  sector employer in dane county wisconsin  well now it faces objections from  hundreds of employees  to its plans to put thousands of workers  back together on its office campus  our consumer investigative correspondent  anna warner along with our partners at  clear health costs  have been learning why some employees  are so upset  well some major tech companies are  keeping their employees working from  home  google through next july twitter  indefinitely  but this giant medical records software  company says all of its employees needed  to come back to the office  and now some of those employees are  speaking to us  nearly 10 thousand people work at epic's  medical records software business on  this sprawling campus outside madison  in mid-march most shifted to working  from home  but now the company says they must come  back  chief administrative officer svarawan we  know that we have this responsibility to  health care workers around the world  and to their patients to make sure that  we are at our very best  the fact is that we can't do what we do  without being together at the absolute  highest level  rawang says to enhance safety on epic's  unique  theme park like campus visited by cbs  late last year  the company took extra steps including  private offices for its employees  enhanced heating and filtration systems  and a take-out only cafeteria  we are not taking this lightly and so we  will do so in a very  mindful and careful way and make sure  that it's safe  but some employees say they don't feel  safe and worry about spreading infection  we've concealed these two employees  identities because they fear being fired  i am deeply morally outraged by  our response i feel like we are not  evaluating the risk that we are taking  i don't want us to be the epicenter of  the next breakout they're reacting to an  email sent to staff earlier this month  by epic ceo judy faulkner obtained by  cbs news  in it she told employees staff working  at home is not nearly as good as staff  working at epic  even if work gets done we are losing big  time the culture that made the company  successful  executives say even workers with high  risk health conditions must return to  the office by november  i have to make the hard choice between  do i risk getting sick or do i risk not  having a paycheck  in internal employee surveys obtained by  cbs news  hundreds of staffers voiced their  objections one writing  i'm quite frankly embarrassed to work at  a company that values employee and  public safety so little  and from another this there will be  people who die  because of epic's insistence on  returning to campus  they described it as dangerous rushed  unnecessary shameful disappointing  wildly irresponsible and a disaster  if the company is doing as good of a job  prioritizing safety with this plan as  you say you  are why are the employees describing it  in that fashion  well i i haven't seen those uh  expressions uh and i hope and  that all of our employees can continue  to work with us  to make sure that we are doing the very  best  by them did you personally read the  surveys  i did and you don't remember these  comments  they we like i said we have a number of  channels that we have  where we get comments uh from employees  including uh  in an email group um all i  was meaning to say is that i don't  recall those words from that survey  but we certainly are hearing the  concerns of our staff just because you  can go back into the office doesn't mean  that you should go back into the office  dr ann ramoin is a ucla professor of  epidemiology  to forfeit safety for the idea of having  collaborative moments is  is something that i just can't agree  with  employees who now have no choice but to  return say they just hope the company  will reconsider  please put the safety of your employees  and the greater community  first now since we spoke with them in an  email to employees late last night  epic ceo said the company is now having  nationally recognized experts review its  plans  and will make adjustments as needed she  also revealed that since the start of  the pandemic  24 staff have tested positive but said  that none of them  said the source came from epic the first  wave of  workers is expected back on monday  yeah it's a tough choice you heard that  employee say having to choose  between getting sick or receiving a  paycheck  our thanks to anna warner for that  reporting

